Alexis  <me@privacy.net> wrote:
>damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk wrote:
>>Alexis, if you're reading this; may I suggest that the client could keep
>>some meta-information in an attempt to detect selective uploading and at
>>least warn against it?

On reflection I mean "only warn against it", since manifestly one could
not detect it with sufficient certainty to enforce it.

>Interesting idea, I'll have to think about it.  The server does do some 
>analysis for save-scumming.  Not sure if there's any easy client-side 
>way to detect selective uploading - by the time the client's been run, 
>if the file's there it'll be uploaded, and if it's not the client has no 
>way of tracking it.

The client could track when it was last run and what bones were then
present in a small logfile. If at runtime 'n' there's a bonesfile present
which is older than runtime 'n-1' but was not seen then, something funny
is going on.

At this point it could, for instance, print a one-line message pointing to
a Web page that discusses these issues in more depth. People who are doing
something odd but legitimate will simply ignore the message; it should be
carefully written so as not to make an accusation.
